Dynamic bottleneck prediction is important to digital manufacturing because timely knowledge of a changing throughput constraint enables production intervention before its effects spread across the line. The task is to determine which resource will constrain throughput and to characterize when the event begins, how long it persists, and what observable production condition explains it. Existing studies are limited by the scarcity of bottleneck events in physical factories and by data that predominantly describe machines while underrepresenting workers, buffers, materials, and logistics. Consequently, conventional time-series models and local graph predictors may not fully capture heterogeneous cross-resource interaction or the delayed propagation of a disturbance into a remote station-level constraint. This paper proposes \textbf{PHGST-DP}, a process-informed heterogeneous-graph spatiotemporal Transformer for fine-grained dynamic bottleneck prediction with delayed propagation. A disturbance-controlled digital production line generates traceable human-, material-, machine-, and logistics-related trajectories without disrupting physical production. Process-informed labelling constructs sustained bottleneck events, while clustering organizes historical operating regimes and precursor patterns; observable process evidence supplies cause targets. A heterogeneous factory graph preserves typed process and logistics relations, while recurrent short-term patterns condition spatiotemporal interaction so that lagged precursors can contribute to prediction. PHGST-DP jointly reports the bottleneck station, onset, duration, process cause, and remaining order time at minute resolution. On the held-out simulation set, report F1 is $80.4\%/77.8\%/76.8\%$ for 5/10/15-min onset caps, with upcoming-event recall near $53\%$. In the archived NoCluster runs, report F1 is $73.4\%/57.0\%/53.7\%$; this comparison also involves different training conditions. A matched-budget auxiliary study improves cause accuracy from $19.5\%$ to $89.5\%$ with cause supervision, while station F1 remains close ($73.5\%$ versus $73.3\%$). Directional entity and delay-pattern ablations further associate heterogeneous observations, cross-type relations, and recurrent pattern conditioning with stronger early warning and process diagnosis. These results support a fine-grained and operationally traceable approach to dynamic bottleneck prediction.

\zhpara{动态瓶颈预测对于数字制造具有重要意义，因为及时获知不断变化的吞吐约束，能够使生产干预在其影响扩散至整条产线之前实施。该任务不仅需要判断未来由哪一资源限制吞吐量，还需要说明事件何时开始、持续多久，以及何种可观测生产状态能够解释该事件。现有研究受到两方面限制：实体工厂中的瓶颈事件样本稀缺；可获取数据又主要描述机器，对人员、缓冲区、物料和物流的表征不足。因此，传统时间序列模型和局部图预测方法难以充分捕捉异构资源之间的交互，以及局部扰动经过一定时延后演变为远端工位约束的过程。为此，本文提出 \textbf{PHGST-DP}，一种面向延迟传播动态瓶颈预测的过程启发式异构图时空 Transformer。可控扰动的数字化生产线在不干扰实体生产的条件下生成可追溯的人员、物料、机器和物流轨迹。过程启发式标签用于构造持续性瓶颈事件，聚类组织历史运行工况与前驱模式，可观测过程证据提供原因预测目标。异构工厂图保留具有类型差异的工艺和物流关系，重复出现的短时模式进一步调节时空交互，使具有时滞的前驱信息能够参与预测。PHGST-DP 以分钟为分辨率，联合报告瓶颈工位、开始时间、持续时间、过程原因和订单剩余时间。在留出仿真测试集上，五/十/十五分钟起点范围的 report F1 分别为 $80.4\%/77.8\%/76.8\%$，upcoming 事件召回率约为 $53\%$。归档 NoCluster 运行的 report F1 为 $73.4\%/57.0\%/53.7\%$，该比较同时涉及训练条件变化。相同预算的辅助组件实验表明，原因监督将原因准确率从 $19.5\%$ 提升至 $89.5\%$，而工位 F1 保持接近（$73.5\%$ 与 $73.3\%$）。方向性多实体与延迟模式消融进一步表明，异构观测、跨类型关系和重复模式条件化与更好的提前预警及过程诊断相关。上述结果支持一种细粒度且具有运营可追溯性的动态瓶颈预测方法。}
